  "answers": {
    "netmedex_hybrid_rag": "**Direct experimental evidence (animal)**  \n- In mouse models lacking Th1/Th2 responses, the emergence of IL‑17‑producing RORγt⁺ CD4⁺ T cells drives tissue inflammation and experimental autoimmune disease, establishing IL‑17 as a pathogenic helper‑T subset【PMID:27576555】.  \n- Fate‑mapping and single‑cell profiling of intestinal Th17 cells showed that a homeostatic IL‑17⁺ TCF1⁺ population can be recruited by IL‑23 to generate GM‑CSF⁺ IFN‑γ⁺ CXCR6⁺ effector cells that mediate CNS autoimmunity (experimental autoimmune encephalomyelitis)【PMID:34875227】.\n\n**Human association evidence**  \n- Elevated IL‑17A (and related family members) is repeatedly reported in patients with multiple sclerosis, rheumatoid arthritis, psoriasis, ankylosing spondylitis, systemic lupus erythematosus and other autoimmune conditions【PMID:37670700】【PMID:27576555】.  \n- The clinical success of FDA‑approved IL‑17‑targeting antibodies (secukinumab, ixekizumab, brodalumab, bimekizumab) in psoriasis, psoriatic arthritis, ankylosing spondylitis and hidradenitis suppurativa demonstrates that IL‑17 contributes to disease pathology in humans【PMID:39875232】.\n\n**Testable mechanistic hypotheses (derived from the reviews)**  \n- IL‑17 promotes autoimmunity by recruiting neutrophils and inducing pro‑inflammatory chemokines, and by cooperating with IL‑23 to convert non‑pathogenic Th17 cells into GM‑CSF‑producing, tissue‑destructive effectors【PMID:30891627】【PMID:34875227】.  \n- Post‑translational modifications of RORγt (e.g., ubiquitination, acetylation) modulate IL‑17 production and may represent therapeutic points to dampen IL‑17‑driven inflammation in"
  },
